<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Guelph Bus Stops &mdash; Reference Layer for OSM Editors</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="index.css">
<link rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
      integrity="sha256-p4NxAoJBhIIN+hmNHrzRCf9tD/miZyoHS5obTRR9BMY="
      crossorigin="">
<script defer src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"
        integrity="sha256-20nQCchB9co0qIjJZRGuk2/Z9VM+kNiyxNV1lvTlZBo="
        crossorigin=""></script>
<script defer src="index.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<h1>Guelph Bus Stops</h1>
<p class="lead">
  A reference layer of the bus stops published by the City of Guelph
  &mdash; <strong>{{STOP_COUNT}}</strong> Guelph Transit stops with their stop
  numbers and names &mdash; ready to add to the
  <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a> editors
  <strong>iD</strong> and <strong>JOSM</strong> while you map.
</p>
<p class="meta">City data: {{DATA_DATE}} &middot; tiles rebuilt: {{BUILD_DATE}}</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <a href="{{LICENSE_URL}}">{{LICENSE_NOTICE}}</a>
  Source: <a href="{{DATASET_PAGE}}">Bus Stops</a>.
</div>

<h2>Preview</h2>
<div id="preview-map" data-raster-url="{{RASTER_URL}}"></div>
<p class="meta">
  The raster layer drawn over OpenStreetMap: a blue dot per stop, its stop
  number from zoom 15, and number and name at zoom 17. A hollow ring is a
  pick-up point served only by on-demand transit.
</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <strong>Coverage vs OpenStreetMap:</strong> see which City stops are
  <a href="gaps/">missing from OSM, missing their <code>ref</code>, misplaced
  or named differently</a> &mdash; a mapper's worklist with an interactive map
  and table.
</div>

<h2>Two layers &mdash; which to use</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Vector layer</th>
    <td>Interactive. Click a stop to read its tags
        (<code>ref</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>fixed_route</code>,
        <code>on_demand</code>, <code>objectid</code>).
        Best in <strong>iD</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Raster layer</th>
    <td>Stops, numbers and names drawn directly on the tiles. A readable
        backdrop. Best in <strong>JOSM</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Heads-up:</strong> the number on the layer is the City's stop ID,
  which is what Guelph's OSM stops already carry as <code>ref</code>. At zoom 15&ndash;16 a number that would print over
  another is left off; at zoom 17 every stop carries at least its number, and
  its name where there is room.
</div>

<h2>Add to iD</h2>
<h3>Vector layer (interactive)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the iD editor and press <kbd>U</kbd> for the <em>Map Data</em> panel.</li>
  <li>Under <em>Custom Map Data</em>, open the settings and paste this URL:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL}}</pre>
<p>Zoom in, then click any stop to see its tags in the inspector.</p>

<h3>Raster layer (visible numbers)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the <em>Background</em> panel and choose <em>Custom</em>.</li>
  <li>Paste this URL template:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{RASTER_URL}}</pre>

<h2>Add to JOSM</h2>
<h3>Raster layer (recommended for JOSM)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> and click <strong>+TMS</strong>.</li>
  <li>Paste this entry (the <code>tms[13,17]</code> prefix sets the zoom range;
      JOSM scales it up past z17):</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">tms[13,17]:{{RASTER_URL_JOSM}}</pre>
<p>
  Give it a name such as <em>Guelph Bus Stops</em>, click OK, then enable
  it from the <em>Imagery</em> menu. Note JOSM's placeholder is
  <code>{zoom}</code>, not <code>{z}</code>.
</p>

<h3>Vector layer (optional)</h3>
<p>
  JOSM can also add the vector tiles via <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> &rarr;
  <strong>+MVT</strong>:
</p>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL_JOSM}}</pre>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Every stop the City publishes is kept. The City drops a retired stop
      from its data rather than flagging it, so a stop OSM still has and this
      layer lacks may be gone &mdash; the gap page lists those as
      &ldquo;OSM only&rdquo;.</li>
  <li>Stop names are the City's, verbatim (&ldquo;Edinburgh at Ginger
      northbound&rdquo;), which is also what most OSM stops in Guelph already
      carry.</li>
  <li>Vector tiles are pre-rendered for z12&ndash;19; raster tiles for
      z13&ndash;17 (the editor overzooms them further).</li>
  <li>Tiles are refreshed weekly from the City's service.</li>
  <li>This is a <strong>reference</strong> layer &mdash; verify against survey or
      street-level imagery before copying anything into OpenStreetMap. The
      City's point marks the stop; where the pole actually stands can differ by
      a few metres.</li>
</ul>

